        {
          "type": "prayer",
          "source": "Jesu puer dulcissime, ter millies ave to: cor moerens tu suavissime unguento mulces lato: qui amat te, prosentitur dulcedinum in mare; tuus dum amor cernitur, quis nollet te amare? qui meum te fraterculum nasci fecit pauperculum.",
          "english": "Jesus, sweetest Child, three thousand times hail to Thee! [The printed “ave to” is irregular.]\nThou most sweetly soothest the sorrowing heart with balm spread upon it.\nWhoever loves Thee [the printed verb “prosentitur” is unclear] into a sea of sweetness.\nWhen Thy love is seen, who would not wish to love Thee?\nThat love made Thee, my little Brother, to be born poor.",
          "sourcePages": [
            52
          ],
          "translation": {
            "kind": "prepared",
            "preparedFor": "Sacrum Florilegium",
            "sourcePages": [
              52
            ],
            "review": "source-reading-pending"
          },
          "alignment": "reviewed",
          "id": "matins-b0006",
          "verification": "source-reading-pending",
          "editorialNote": "PDF p.52: the earlier “plunges” supplied an unverified meaning for “prosentitur”. This verb remains unresolved. The closing relative clause refers to the love just mentioned; the printed Latin is preserved.",
          "originalPreparedEnglish": "Jesus, sweetest Child, three thousand times hail to Thee!\nWith Thy balm Thou most sweetly soothest the sorrowing heart.\nWhoever loves Thee plunges into a sea of sweetness.\nWhen Thy love is seen, who would not wish to love Thee,\nWhom it made to be born poor, as my little Brother?",
          "originalTranslation": {
            "kind": "prepared",
            "preparedFor": "Sacrum Florilegium",
            "sourcePages": [
              52
            ],
            "review": "editorial-review-complete"
          }
