        {
          "type": "prayer",
          "source": "Fleo puer ac rideo, ridens cogor plorare tuisdum stellas video in oculis micare: candor ostrum eximitum rosarum genis nectit, ac lux corusca nivium se artubus implectit: auro dant crispo splendicant crines, cor sibi vindicant.",
          "english": "O Child, I weep and smile; smiling, I am compelled to weep\nWhen I see stars shining in Thine eyes.\nFairness binds the exquisite crimson of roses upon Thy cheeks,\nAnd the gleaming brightness of snow enfolds Thy limbs.\n[The printed “auro dant crispo splendicant” is irregular; its exact sense is unresolved.]\nThy hair claims the heart for itself.",
          "sourcePages": [
            53
          ],
          "translation": {
            "kind": "prepared",
            "preparedFor": "Sacrum Florilegium",
            "sourcePages": [
              53
            ],
            "review": "source-reading-pending"
          },
          "alignment": "reviewed",
          "id": "sext-b0004",
          "verification": "source-reading-pending",
          "editorialNote": "PDF p.53: the earlier curling golden hair translation silently repaired “auro dant crispo splendicant”. The uncertain clause is now identified in the English. “Candor” is translated as fairness rather than supplying “Thy beauty”; printed Latin is unchanged.",
          "originalPreparedEnglish": "O Child, I weep and smile; smiling, I am moved to weep\nWhen I see stars shining in Thine eyes.\nThy fair beauty sets the exquisite crimson of roses upon Thy cheeks,\nAnd the gleaming brightness of snow enfolds Thy limbs.\nThy curling hair shines like gold\nAnd claims the heart for itself.",
          "originalTranslation": {
            "kind": "prepared",
            "preparedFor": "Sacrum Florilegium",
            "sourcePages": [
              53
            ],
            "review": "editorial-review-complete"
          }
        },
